/**
@mainpage FyuuRHI Object Guide

@tableofcontents

FyuuRHI objects are created in this direction:

@code
Instance -> PhysicalDevice -> LogicalDevice
                              |- Resource -> View
                              |- Sampler
                              |- Pipeline -> PipelineResourceGroup
                              `- CommandScheduler -> CommandGraphBuilder
                                                   -> CommandGraphBindings
                                                   -> CommandGraphResources
@endcode

Except for Instance and CommandScheduler, GPU objects have unique ownership and
are move-only. Never mix objects created by different Backend values or different
LogicalDevice objects. A started command graph temporarily owns every bound
object and returns them to its receiver on success, failure, or cancellation.

@section context Context and diagnostics

Call fyuu_rhi::InitializeRHIContext before using any backend. Application and
engine names and versions become backend metadata. The application owns the
fyuu_rhi::log::Sink; it must outlive all RHI calls and accept synchronous calls
from recording and completion threads.

@code
fyuu_rhi::InitializeRHIContext(
    "Application",
    { 0u, 1u, 0u, 0u },
    "Engine",
    { 0u, 1u, 0u, 0u },
    &sink
);
@endcode

@section instance Instance

fyuu_rhi::EnumerateBackends returns the backends compiled into this build. Pass
one returned value to fyuu_rhi::RequestInstance. The function directly returns
an fyuu_rhi::Instance reference to singleton state retained for the process
lifetime by FyuuRHI.

@code
auto& instance = fyuu_rhi::RequestInstance(fyuu_rhi::Backend::Vulkan);
auto physical_devices = instance.EnumeratePhysicalDevices();
@endcode

- EnumeratePhysicalDevices() returns the adapters exposed by that backend.
- ShareContextOnThisThread() makes the shared OpenGL context current on an
  additional thread; it is a no-op for other backends.
- Instance exposes no native API handle and requires no visitor.

@section physical_device PhysicalDevice

fyuu_rhi::PhysicalDevice is a move-only adapter object. GetInfo() returns its
name and class plus optional vendor ID, device ID, and dedicated-memory size.
An empty optional means that the backend cannot report the value, not that the
value is zero. fyuu_rhi::BestPerformance selects from a non-empty span by device
class and memory. CreateLogicalDevice() establishes the ownership domain for all
objects that will be used together.

@section logical_device LogicalDevice

fyuu_rhi::LogicalDevice is a move-only factory. It creates buffers, textures,
immutable samplers, graphics and compute pipelines, and copyable command
schedulers. Resources, views, samplers, pipelines, and resource groups from two
logical devices cannot be placed in the same execution or bound to each other.

@section resource Resource and creation flags

fyuu_rhi::Resource is a move-only buffer or texture. Resource creation flags
must declare every future use: memory visibility, copy directions, buffer roles,
texture roles, dimension, allowed view types and aspects, sample count, and
format. These flags are correctness requirements, not optimization hints.

@code
fyuu_rhi::ResourceFlags flags;
flags.Set(fyuu_rhi::ResourceFlagBits::HostVisible);
flags.Set(fyuu_rhi::ResourceFlagBits::CopyDST);
flags.Set(fyuu_rhi::ResourceFlagBits::UniformBuffer);
auto buffer = device.CreateBuffer(4096u, flags);
@endcode

A texture normally selects exactly one dimension, format, sample count, memory
policy, and the binding, attachment, or copy capabilities it needs.
GetBufferSize() applies only to buffers, GetTextureExtent() only to textures,
and GetFlags() returns the immutable creation contract.

@section view View

fyuu_rhi::View is a move-only interpretation of part of a Resource; it does not
replace Resource ownership. Create it through Resource::CreateBufferView() or
Resource::CreateTextureView(). Its range must fit the resource and its flags
must be allowed by the resource's creation flags. Commands commonly bind both a
Resource for access/state tracking and a View for the native view description.

@section sampler Sampler

fyuu_rhi::Sampler is move-only immutable sampling state. SamplerDescriptor
specifies U/V/W addressing, magnification/minification/mipmap filtering,
anisotropy, comparison, and the LOD interval. A maximum anisotropy of one
disables anisotropic filtering; an Unknown comparison function selects ordinary
non-comparison sampling.

@section pipeline Pipeline

fyuu_rhi::Pipeline is a move-only graphics or compute pipeline. Spans in
SlangPipelineProgramDescriptor are borrowed only during creation. Modules,
entry points, macros, include paths, optimization, and matrix layout produce
backend shaders and the common reflected binding ABI.

GraphicsPipelineDescriptor also specifies vertex buffers and attributes,
topology, rasterization, multisampling, depth/stencil state, and the format,
blend state, and write mask of each color attachment. BeginRendering attachments
must be compatible with these declarations. ComputePipelineDescriptor must
describe a compute program.

@section resource_group PipelineResourceGroup and BindingValue

Pipeline::CreatePipelineResourceGroup(space, bindings) materializes one
reflected shader space/set. Supply every required slot and array element exactly
once:

- BindingValue::FromBuffer records a buffer, base byte offset, and range.
- BindingValue::FromView binds an independent texture or storage view.
- BindingValue::FromSampler binds an independent sampler.
- BindingValue::FromCombined binds one logical combined texture and sampler.

The C++ references in BindingValue are borrowed only during group creation.
Each backend retains the native ownership required to keep its descriptors
valid. PipelineResourceGroup::Space() returns the creation space.

Dynamic buffer binding does not mutate the group. BindResourceGroup names the
same space and supplies additional_buffer_offsets ordered by `(slot,
array_element)`. Only direct buffer bindings consume entries; textures and
samplers do not. An empty array means zero additional offsets. Each effective
range begins at `base offset + additional offset` and must meet resource bounds
and the backend's uniform/storage alignment. The same immutable group can be
reused with different offsets before different draws or dispatches.

@section scheduler CommandScheduler and command graphs

fyuu_rhi::execution::CommandScheduler is copyable; copies share scheduling
state. schedule() starts a CommandGraphBuilder without reserving a physical
queue. RegisterResource/View/Sampler/Pipeline/ResourceGroup create typed slots.
Commands store these slot numbers; actual objects are moved into the operation
after connect().

CreateNode(queue, dependency) creates a node. QueueType is a capability request,
not a promise of a dedicated hardware queue. Node::DependsOn adds a directed
edge, Node::Access declares a ResourceRange, AccessMode, and ResourceUsage, and
Node::Record appends commands in node-local order. Access declarations must
accurately cover the commands: the scheduler derives topological order,
batches, state transitions, queue-family ownership transfers, and cross-queue
synchronization from them.

@section commands Command records

- BeginRendering / EndRendering open and close a render scope and describe its
  area, load/store operations, clear values, resolve attachments, and optional
  depth/stencil attachment.
- BindPipeline selects a registered graphics or compute pipeline.
- BindResourceGroup selects a matching space and optional dynamic buffer offsets.
- SetPipelineConstants writes one reflected immediate-constant range. slot and
  space select the range; offset is relative to its start; offset and data size
  are four-byte aligned.
- BindVertexBuffer / BindIndexBuffer select buffers and their stride/type and
  base offsets.
- Viewport / Scissor set rasterization bounds. Viewport::clip_space states the
  application's NDC Y orientation.
- Draw / DrawIndexed issue ordinary or indexed instanced rendering.
- Dispatch submits an X/Y/Z compute workgroup grid.
- CopyBuffer, CopyBufferToTexture, CopyTextureToBuffer, and CopyTexture copy
  declared ranges.
- WriteBuffer writes CPU data into a host-visible buffer with matching creation
  capabilities.
- Present sends a source texture to a presentation-target slot.

ResourceUsage must match the command role: examples include VertexBuffer,
CopyDestination, ColorAttachment, and PresentationSource. A monostate
ResourceRange means the whole resource. Zero size/count in a concrete
BufferRange or TextureRange is invalid and does not mean the whole resource.

@section completion Binding, execution, and ownership return

Move a finished builder into connect(receiver) to obtain CommandGraphBindings.
Bind every registered slot exactly once with the corresponding BindResource,
BindView, BindSampler, BindPipeline, or BindResourceGroup function. Fill every
Present target through SetPresentationTarget, then call start().

The moveable receiver provides get_env() and these noexcept operations:

- `RecoverBindings(CommandGraphResources&&)`;
- `set_value(CommandGraphResources&&) &&`;
- `set_error(std::exception_ptr) &&`;
- `set_stopped() &&`.

Success delivers owned objects to set_value. Failure or cancellation first
returns them through RecoverBindings and then sends one terminal signal.
CommandGraphResources::TakeResource/View/Sampler/Pipeline/ResourceGroup uses the
original slot number and consumes each slot once. Completion may occur on an
internal worker thread, so receivers must synchronize shared application state.

CompletionToken is move-only backend submission state. Poll() is non-blocking,
Error() returns a captured failure, and IsStopped() reports cancellation. Most
sender users receive completion through the receiver instead of managing a
token directly.

@section presentation Platform handles and presentation

PlatformHandle is HWND on Windows, ANativeWindow* on Android, an X11/Wayland
variant on Linux, and CA::MetalLayer* on Apple platforms. The application owns
the window, event loop, resize handling, and close flow. Present::target refers
to a SetPresentationTarget slot, not to a resource slot. One graph may present
to several distinct targets.

@section errors Errors and stopping

Preparation reports missing bindings, invalid ranges, and backend/device mixing
before native submission. Recording and submission failures use the receiver's
error channel. StopTokenView is observed during preparation and recording;
already-submitted GPU work generally cannot be cancelled. Backend diagnostics
are also written synchronously to the configured log::Sink.
*/
